<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virapurushadatta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century CE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Virapurushadatta was a king of the Ikshvaku dynasty of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 3rd century CE, the son of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasishthiputra-chamtamula">Vasishthiputra Chamtamula</a>. His reign, known from inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda">Nagarjunakonda</a>, saw the height of gifts to Buddhist foundations at the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayapuri">Vijayapuri</a>, made largely by women of the royal family. He married a princess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>.</p></div>
